+PLACE THE OPEN GUIDE AT THE EXPOSED CANDIDATE: Use a boundary made visible or more accessible by the peel, with a current supported jaw approach and clearance from the table/partner. Compare its relative motion and continuity with the anchored region; do not require certification that it is the opposite fabric layer. Color, a dark fold, depth alone and a background patch do not identify hidden lips. Align the open jaw and its approach using current views, then make a bounded placement and inspect. A shallow scoop is only a candidate when its gap and above-table approach are already observable; never wedge beneath unseen layers or push into the table to search for a presumed boundary. Brief jaw occlusion alone is not failure, but reobserve or recover when the approach relationship becomes untrackable. If the peel has not exposed an accessible candidate, do not silently skip exposure and call an arbitrary placement this method.
